# The GOLF/2 compiler, written in expanded GOLF (M-SELF).
#
# THIS IS THE COMPILER'S SOURCE.  Since M-SELF, compiler logic is written here,
# in the language the compiler itself grew: named variables (→x / ←x) instead of
# stack juggling, one op per line, every op explained.
#
# Build: tools/mkblob2.py code-page encodes this file and splices the template
# blob in at the @BLOB@ marker below (a backtick, the byte count, one space, the
# raw bytes), producing the GENERATED self/golf2.golf.  Never hand-edit that
# file; run `python3 tools/mkblob2.py` from expanded/.  The ladder in
# test/run2.sh then closes the loop:
#
#     golf0.py -> v1c -> seed (self/seed.golf) -> golf2 -> golf2'
#                                                 ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
#                                                 fixpoint: golf2 == golf2'
#
# self/seed.golf carries a semantically IDENTICAL copy of this compiler written
# in v1 GOLF (v1's mkblob.WORDS, the spliced compiler cases REF_CASE / STR_CASE /
# SET_CASE / GET_CASE / CHAIN_CASE / LINK_CASE, a `;` that closes a chain as well
# as a definition, and the word X), purely so v1c — which has never heard of v2 —
# can bootstrap this one.  Same token dispatch order, same backpatch discipline,
# same emitted bytes for every input; test/selfcheck.sh gates that byte for
# byte, so a change to compiler logic must land in BOTH or selfcheck goes red.
#
# Only ops the SEED can compile may appear below: ASCII, the compiler prefixes
# (′ “ → ← ⊚) and the atom bytes.  selfcheck.sh enforces it.  Note that this
# source names those bytes as decimal literals (140, 176, …) rather than writing
# the glyphs, so the constraint has never actually bitten.
#
# The blob is still a raw-byte template table and still must be spliced in by
# the build.  Splice it AFTER encoding, into the raw byte stream: the blob is
# arbitrary bytes, and a `#` inside it would eat the rest of the line if the
# encoder ever saw it.  Everything else here is hand-written source.
#
# Comments: tools/codepage.py encode DROPS `#` to end of line before the bytes
# ever reach a compiler, so a comment may contain anything at all — backslashes,
# glyphs, non-code-page characters.  Only the newline survives, as whitespace.
#
# Layout: one op per line, except that a literal shares a line with the op that
# consumes it (`4+`, `233o`, `1<`) — that pair reads as one idea.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime memory (the compiler's own process; a compiled program never sees it)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   0x410034   (m+52) a 4-byte staging cell: `e` builds the two bytes of its
#              undefined-word diagnostic here (the name, then a newline), the
#              only fixed address this compiler writes outside the three below
#   0x4E0000   variable bank: cell for name x is at 0x4E0000+8*x  (→x / ←x)
#   0x480000   long-name table (M2): the name with number E (256..1279) has
#              a 64-byte entry at 0x47C000+64*E — [length:4][up to 60 bytes];
#              E = 1280 (0x490000) is scratch: ‹readname› reads a name into
#              the next free entry before it knows the name is new
#   0x490400   forward-reference heads: the cell for name N (0..1279, one-byte
#              names included since W10-A) is at 0x490400+4*N and holds the
#              last unpatched site, 0 = none
#   0x492000   dictionary: the body address of the word named N is at
#              0x492000+4*N, N = 0..1279 (W10-A moved it up from v1's D+2048,
#              where 256 one-byte and 256 long slots were all that fit below
#              the output buffer)
#   0x494000   output buffer B: the byte at file offset f is stored at B+f;
#              196608 bytes (192 KB, the program-size cap) plus slack up to
#              the thunk arena at 0x4D0000 (W11-1E; it was v1's D+4096 =
#              0x411000, which had room for 64 KB)
#
# None of these regions is ever seen by a compiled program, so moving one
# changes no emitted byte.  A target virtual address is bufaddr - 606208
# (= file offset + 0x400000).
#
# The compiler's scalar state lives in the variable bank instead of v1's D+0..
# D+28 cells.  It owns its whole process — user programs are *compiled*, never
# run in-process — so the bank is free real estate here.  (lib/prelude.golfj may
# never do this: there the bank is user space.)
#
#   p  output write cursor (an absolute address inside B)   was D+4
#   i  current input char                                   was D+0
#   T  template blob base address                           was D+8
#   q  pushed-back input char, 0 = none                     was D+16
#   n  the record key `f` is scanning for                   was D+12
#   b  the name/op byte the current handler is working on   (new)
#   s  `k`'s backpatch site                                 (new)
#   d  `c`'s copy source address                            (new)
#   m  `c`'s remaining byte count                           (new)
#   R  a blob record's address (`C`, `H`)                   (new)
#   A  a walking byte pointer (`H`, the final flush)        (new)
#   z  one past the last header (offset,value) pair         was D+12
#   v  the virtual address of an emitted data block         was D+12
#   j  a pending jmp-over site (′ thunk, “ block)           was D+20
#   r  the address of a ′ thunk                             was D+24
#   l  the address of a “ block's length field              was D+24
#   x  a “ block's byte count                               was D+28
#   y  `Z`'s digit temporary                                (new)
#   S  `e`'s undefined-word message block                   (new)
#   J  1 while compiling a ⊡ definition, 0 otherwise         (M-FRAME)
#   u  1 while compiling a chain body, 0 otherwise           (M-CHAIN2)
#   K  how many links the chain has taken so far             (M-CHAIN2)
#   F  the 1st buffered link byte                            (M-CHAIN2)
#   G  the 2nd buffered link byte                            (M-CHAIN2)
#   I  the 3rd buffered link byte                            (M-CHAIN2)
#
# M2's state has LONG names, because the one-letter pool is spent — every letter
# is already a word or a variable here, and this file keeps the two apart:
#
#   ‹count›  how many long names have been interned
#   ‹entry›  the table entry a long name is being read into
#   ‹len›    its length so far
#   ‹ch›     the byte just read into it
#   ‹idx›    an index walking the long-name table
#   ‹other›  the entry ‹same› compares against, or ‹die›'s name entry
#   ‹left›   ‹same›'s remaining byte count
#   ‹head›   a forward-reference head cell
#   ‹site›   ‹resolve›'s current site, ‹next› the one after it on the list
#
# M-FRAME2 (W9-4D) adds two more, live for the span of one ⊡ definition:
#
#   ‹locals› how many distinct locals the definition has named so far
#   ‹frame›  the last unpatched frame-size immediate (the head of its list)
#
# and one compiler-only table outside the bank: the name number of local slot i
# is the 4-byte cell at m+96+4*i = 4259936+4*i, i < 16 (../REGISTRY.md §3).
#
# No two of these are ever live at the same time in different roles; the notes
# above are the whole allocation.  Word names and variable names are separate
# namespaces (dictionary vs bank), but none is reused across the two here.
# M-CHAIN2's five are the exception to "no two are live at once": they are live
# together, for exactly the span of one chain definition, and the seed holds the
# same five in its own scratch at m32..m48 (../REGISTRY.md §3).
#
# Words, in definition-before-use order: B Y Z U ‹say› ‹toolarge› a o L w W k c C f E V
# ‹die› ‹badname› ‹same› ‹readname› ‹getname› ‹forward› ‹resolve› ‹undefined›
# e D g h X ‹badlocal› ‹slot› t H.  The M2 words have long names for the same reason their state
# does — and so the compiler is its own first user of the feature: the seed
# implements long names too, and builds this file.
#
# LONG NAMES (M2).  `‹name›` (bytes 0xB4 … 0xB5) is a name of any length, in
# every place a one-byte name can stand: `:‹sq›`, `⊚‹sq›`, `⊡‹sq›`, a call
# `‹sq›`, `′‹sq›`, `→‹n›`, `←‹n›`.  `‹readname›` interns it to a NAME NUMBER 256+index,
# and from there on it is just a name that does not fit a byte: the dictionary
# slot `Y` computes and the variable cell `→`/`←` compute are the same formulas,
# one past the byte-indexed ranges (0x492400.. and 0x4E0800..).  So a program
# that never writes ‹ compiles to exactly the bytes it always did.  Up to 1024
# long names per program (W10-A; it was 256).
#
# FORWARD REFERENCES, for every name (M2 for long names, W10-A for one-byte
# ones): a call to a name not yet defined emits `call rel32` with the name's
# pending-site list threaded through the placeholder (‹forward›), the definition
# walks and patches that list (‹resolve›), and anything still pending at end of
# input is `GOLF: undefined word <name>`, exit 2, before a byte is written
# (‹undefined›).  So mutual recursion needs no declaration.  A name only goes
# forward when it is not defined yet: a template byte is still a template, and
# a call after a definition binds to it directly, so a word redefined later
# keeps its earlier callers on the earlier body.
#
# Every value the compiler handles is an address inside the load segment (under
# 0x600000) or a small integer, and it installs no vectorization hooks (its hook
# cells stay BSS-zero), so the bare + - * take the scalar path here, byte for
# byte.  The raw atoms (﹢ ﹣ ﹡) would compile to the same code and are not needed.
#
# Named variables also removed every single stack-juggling op: this source
# contains no swap, no over, and `"` dup only where a value is genuinely tested
# and kept.  v1's P/B/S/Q accessor words are gone with them.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Constants
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:B          # B : the output buffer's base address              [ -> addr ]
4800512     # 0x494000 (W11-1E; v1's D+4096 = 0x411000 held only 64 KB).
;           # File offset f is held at B+f.

:Y          # Y : the dictionary slot of a word name            [ name -> addr ]
4*          # one 4-byte cell per name ...
4792320+    # ... in the table at 0x492000 (W10-A; v1's was at 0x410800)
;

:Z          # Z : fold one decimal digit into an accumulator  [ acc, ch -> acc' ]
→y          # park the digit character
10*         # shift the accumulator up one decimal place
←y+         # add the character ...
48-         # ... and subtract '0' to get its value
;

:U          # U : is-digit test core                       [ ch -> -1 if digit ]
48-         # ch - '0'
10<         # unsigned < 10, so a non-digit gives 0 (and `[` fires on 0)
;


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Emitting bytes into the output buffer
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:‹say›      # ‹say› : write a “…“ block to fd 2                  [ msgblock -> ]
→S          # park the block; its address is needed twice
2           # fd 2
←S
4+          # the bytes start after the block's 4-byte length field ...
←S
@           # ... which is the length
1⎈          # write(2, msg, len)
_
;

# W11-1E: the program-size cap.  A compiled program is ONE PT_LOAD at
# 0x400000 and its image may not reach 0x430000, the lowest address a compiled
# program uses at run time (W11-1C's output buffer, ../REGISTRY.md §3): at most
# 196608 bytes = 192 KB, header included.  `a` is the only word that moves the
# cursor, so it is where the cap is checked — before the buffer can run past
# B+192 KB into anything the compiler itself still needs (the largest single
# advance is one 255-byte template, and 0x4C4000..0x4CFFFF is slack).

:‹toolarge› # ‹toolarge› : the output passed the cap               [ -> !!! ]
“GOLF: program too large“
‹say›
10          # a newline, staged in m+52
4259892!
2
4259892
1
1⎈
_
2.          # exit(2); nothing has been written to fd 1
;

:a          # a : advance the write cursor by n                    [ n -> ]
←p
+
"
→p
4997121     # B + 196608 + 1: the cursor may stand AT the cap, not past it
<
[           # not below it: too large
‹toolarge›
]
;

:o          # o : emit one byte                                    [ b -> ]
←p          # a 32-bit store of a byte value writes three zeros past the cursor;
!           # the buffer is zero-filled and the next emit overwrites them, so
1a          # only one byte is ever claimed
;

:L          # L : emit the push-imm32 opcode                       [ -> ]
104o        # 0x68
;

:w          # w : emit a 32-bit little-endian dword                [ v -> ]
←p
!
4a
;

# W : emit the push of a constant, in whichever width the constant needs (M4).
#
# `push imm32` is one byte shorter but SIGN-EXTENDS its operand, so it can only
# carry 0..2^31-1: 4294967296 would arrive as -4294967296 and 2147483648 as
# -2147483648.  A value that does not fit goes through rax instead —
#
#     48 B8 <imm64>   mov rax, <the full 64-bit constant>
#     50              push rax
#
# — the only x86-64 form that can carry a full 64-bit immediate.  The test is
# `v >> 31`: it is 0 exactly for the values push imm32 reproduces, so every
# literal that fitted before still compiles to exactly the bytes it did before
# and only genuinely wide ones pay the extra 6.
#
# The accumulator this reads was always a 64-bit register (`10*` is imul rax),
# so nothing above had to change — only the emitter at the end of the number.
#
# `w` stores through the 32-bit `!`, i.e. it writes the low dword of whatever it
# is handed; the second `w` is therefore fed `v >> 32` explicitly.

:W          # W : emit `push <v>`, 32- or 64-bit wide              [ v -> ]
"           # keep v; the copy decides the width
31>         # v >> 31 : 0 exactly when v fits a sign-extended imm32
[           # it fits ->
L           #   0x68            push imm32
w           #   the value, as one dword
^
]
72o         # 0x48 0xB8       mov rax, imm64
184o
"
w           # its low dword ...
32>
w           # ... and its high dword
80o         # 0x50            push rax
;

:k          # k : backpatch a pending rel32 site to land here      [ site -> ]
→s          # the site was left on the data stack by ':' '[' or '′'
←p
←s
-
4-          # rel32 = p - (site + 4)
←s
!
;


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The template blob: [key][len][len bytes] records, ending in a 0 key sentinel
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:c          # c : copy n bytes from an address to the output   [ addr, n -> ]
→m          # remaining count
→d          # source cursor
{
←d
?           # read one source byte
o           # and emit it
←d
1+
→d
←m
1-
→m
←m
1<          # loop while the count has not reached 0
}
;

:C          # C : copy a record's template body                    [ rec -> ]
→R
←R
2+          # the body starts after the [key][len] pair
←R
1+
?           # its length is the record's second byte
c
;

:f          # f : the record for a key, or the sentinel     [ key -> rec addr ]
→n          # remember what we are scanning for
←T          # start at the first record
{
"
?           # this record's key byte
"
[           # 0 -> the sentinel: the key is not in the blob
_
^           #   return the sentinel's address
]
←n
-
[           # keys match ->
^           #   return this record's address
]
"
1+
?           # this record's body length
2+          # plus its [key][len] pair
+           # step to the next record
0           # and keep scanning
}
;

:E          # E : emit the template stored under a key             [ key -> ]
f
C
;


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compiling one ordinary token
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

:V          # V : report a compile error and stop            [ msgblock -> !!! ]
            # Writes `<msg> <the byte in b>` to fd 2 and exits 2.  fd 2, never
            # fd 1: fd 1 is carrying the binary.  Every caller has already put
            # the offending byte in `b`, which is where the handlers keep the
            # name they are working on anyway.  A word rather than three copies
            # of the same fifteen ops — the compiler is small on purpose.
‹say›       # the message
←b
2560+       # the offending byte, with a newline (10<<8) stacked above it
4259892!    # staged in the compiler's own scratch cell at m+52
2
4259892
2
1⎈          # write(2, m+52, 2) — the byte, then the newline
_
2.          # exit(2).  No output file is worth completing.
;


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Long names (M2): ‹name›
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A long name is interned into a 1024-entry table at its first appearance and
# is a NAME NUMBER 256+index from then on.  Entry E (E = 256..1279) is the 64
# bytes at 0x47C000 + 64*E — [length:4][the name, at most 60 bytes] — and any
# name N's forward-reference head is the cell at 0x490400 + 4*N.  Both regions
# are compiler-only (../REGISTRY.md §3); a compiled program never sees them.

:‹die›      # ‹die› : `<msg>‹name›` + newline to fd 2, exit 2   [ msg entry -> !!! ]
→‹other›    # the name's table entry
‹say›
12157154    # 0xB980E2: the three UTF-8 bytes of ‹, staged in m+52.  A string
4259892!    # literal would put high bytes in this source that selfcheck.sh's
2           # seed-compilability check (rightly) refuses, so they are a number
4259892
3
1⎈
_
2
←‹other›
4﹢          # the name's bytes ...
←‹other›
@           # ... and its length
1⎈
_
179994850   # 0x0ABA80E2: ›, then a newline
4259892!
2
4259892
4
1⎈
_
2.
;

:‹badname›  # ‹badname› : reject the long name being read     [ -> !!! ]
←‹len›      # what was read of it so far is what gets shown
←‹entry›
!
“GOLF: bad long name “
←‹entry›
‹die›
;

:‹same›     # ‹same› : 0 iff entries ‹other› and ‹entry› hold one name [ -> f ]
←‹len›      # compare the length cell AND the bytes, last to first: names of
4﹢          # different lengths differ in that cell, so ‹ab› never matches ‹abc›
→‹left›
0           # a dummy for the first `_` below
{
_           # drop the last byte difference (0, or the dummy)
←‹left›
[           # nothing left to compare: the same name
0
^
]
←‹left›
1﹣
→‹left›
←‹other›
←‹left›
﹢
?
←‹entry›
←‹left›
﹢
?
﹣           # the byte difference: 0 keeps scanning,
"           # anything else leaves the loop with it on the stack
}
;

:‹readname› # ‹readname› : read a long name after ‹, intern it  [ -> 256+index ]
←‹count›
256﹢
64﹡
4702208﹢
→‹entry›    # read it straight into the next free entry: a new name is then
0           # interned just by counting it, with no copy
→‹len›
{
(
"
181﹣
[           # › : the name is complete
_
←‹len›
←‹entry›
!           #   its length goes in the entry's first cell
←‹len›
[           #   ‹› is not a name
‹badname›
]
0
→‹idx›
{           #   look for it among the names seen so far
←‹idx›
←‹count›
﹣
[           #     not one of them: a new name
←‹count›
1024<
[           #       1024 long names fill the table
“GOLF: too many long names “
←‹entry›
‹die›
]
←‹count›
"
1﹢
→‹count›    #       claim the entry it was read into
256﹢
^
]
←‹idx›
256﹢
64﹡
4702208﹢
→‹other›
‹same›
[           #     the same name: its number
←‹idx›
256﹢
^
]
←‹idx›
1﹢
→‹idx›
0
}
]
→‹ch›       # an ordinary byte of the name
←‹ch›
48﹣
10<         # 0-9
←‹ch›
65﹣
26<         # A-Z
﹢
←‹ch›
97﹣
26<         # a-z
﹢
←‹ch›
95﹣
1<          # _
﹢
←‹ch›
45﹣
1<          # -
﹢
[           # -1 for exactly one of those, 0 for anything else — EOF included
‹badname›
]
←‹len›
60<
[           # an entry holds 60 bytes of name
‹badname›
]
←‹ch›
←‹entry›
4﹢
←‹len›
﹢
,           # store the byte
←‹len›
1﹢
→‹len›
0
}
;

:‹getname›  # ‹getname› : a prefix's operand                    [ -> name ]
(           # one byte, as it always was ...
"
180﹣
[           # ... unless it is ‹ : then the whole long name
_
‹readname›
]
;

:‹forward›  # ‹forward› : emit `op rel32` to a name not yet defined [ op -> ]
o           # the opcode: 0xE8 call, or 0xE9 inside a ′ thunk
←b
4﹡
4785152﹢
→‹head›     # name b's list of pending sites
←p          # this site joins it: the placeholder holds the old head ...
←‹head›
@
w
←‹head›
!           # ... and the head is now this site
;

:‹resolve›  # ‹resolve› : patch name b's pending sites to its body [ -> ]
←b          # A program that only calls backwards never has any, so this is a
4﹡          # no-op for it: it compiles to exactly the bytes it did before
4785152﹢    # forward references existed (M2, W10-A).
→‹head›
←‹head›
@
→‹site›
0
←‹head›
!           # the list is consumed
{
←‹site›
[
^           # the end of the list
]
←‹site›
@
→‹next›     # the placeholder holds the next site
←b
Y
@
←‹site›
﹣
4﹣          # rel32 = body - (site + 4)
←‹site›
!
←‹next›
→‹site›
0
}
;

:‹undefined› # ‹undefined› : reject a name still pending at end of input [ -> ]
0           # walk every name number: the 256 one-byte names, then the long
→‹idx›      # names interned so far (W10-A; M2 walked only the long ones)
{
←‹idx›
←‹count›
256﹢
﹣
[           # every name checked
^
]
←‹idx›
4﹡
4785152﹢
@           # its pending-site list
1<
[           # not empty: called, but never defined
“GOLF: undefined word “
←‹idx›
256<
1﹢
[           #   a one-byte name: V shows the byte, as the old immediate check did
←‹idx›
→b
V
]
←‹idx›      #   a long name: ‹die› shows it in full
64﹡
4702208﹢
‹die›
]
←‹idx›
1﹢
→‹idx›
0
}
;

:e          # e : compile a table op, or a call to a defined word  [ ch -> ]
→b
←b
f           # look the byte up in the template blob
"
?
[           # the sentinel: no template, so this is a word call
_           #   drop the sentinel's address
←b
Y
@           #   the word's body address, from the dictionary ...
"
[           #   ... which is 0 for a name not defined YET.  Emitting the call
            #   anyway is what v1 did: rel32 = 0 - (p+4) is a jump to a wild
            #   address, so the compile REPORTED SUCCESS and the binary died
            #   with SIGSEGV the moment the word was reached.  M-DIAG made it
            #   an immediate error; M2 (long names) and W10-A (one-byte names)
            #   let the name come later instead — that is how mutual recursion
            #   is written — and ‹undefined› rejects it at end of input if it
            #   never does.
_
232         #     call it through its pending-site list, which its
‹forward›   #     definition patches
^
]
232o        #   0xE8 = call rel32
←p
4+
-           #   rel32 = body - (p + 4)
w
^
]
C           # a template: copy its body verbatim
;


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reading input
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

